sec.p:
ρorig=14(ρ(X1,Y1)+ρ(X2,Y2)+ρ(X3,Y3)+ρ(X4,Y4)).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \rho_{\text {orig }}=\frac{1}{4}\left(\rho\left(X_{1}, Y_{1}\right)+\rho\left(X_{2}, Y_{2}\right)+\rho\left(X_{3}, Y_{3}\right)+\rho\left(X_{4}, Y_{4}\right)\right).$$\end{document}



Next, as a null model, recompute the mean correlation after permuting the Y time series while holding the X time series in the original order. For instance, one such permutation might be:


ρshuffle=14(ρ(X1,Y3)+ρ(X2,Y2)+ρ(X3,Y4)+ρ(X4,Y1)).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \rho_{\text {shuffle }}=\frac{1}{4}\left(\rho\left(X_{1}, Y_{3}\right)+\rho\left(X_{2}, Y_{2}\right)+\rho\left(X_{3}, Y_{4}\right)+\rho\left(X_{4}, Y_{1}\right)\right).$$\end{document}



A p-value can be calculated as the proportion of permutations (including the original ordering) that produce a mean correlation that is as strong as, or stronger than, ρorig (Fig 1A). One may then detect a correlation at the significance level α if p ≤ α. We emphasize that these permutations are obtained by swapping trials, not by swapping time points. This test has been used to detect correlations between time series in neuroscience and psychology settings [20, 18, 21]. It has also been used to detect correlations between variables measured in brain images, which can have similar nonstationarity challenges as time series [22]. A noteworthy advantage of this approach is that it is valid (meaning that the false positive rate is guaranteed to not exceed α) under very mild assumptions. Namely, the test is valid if the Xi trials are exchangeable with one another (i.e. all permutations of the sequence X1, …, Xn have the same joint probability distribution), or if the Yi trials are exchangeable (see Corollary 4 in Appendix 1).

Analogously, if X = (X1, …, Xn) and Y = (Y1, …, Yn) are collections of time series with n trials each, and if ρ is some correlation function, we say that an X-perfect match has occurred if (and only if) for each i ∈ {1, …, n},
ρ(Xi,Yi)>ρ(Xi,Yj)for allj≠i. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \rho\left(X_i, Y_i\right)>\rho\left(X_i, Y_j\right) \text { for all } j \neq i .$$\end{document}
Similarly, a Y-perfect match has occurred if and only if for each i,
ρ(Xi,Yi)>ρ(Xj,Yi)for allj≠i. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \rho\left(X_i, Y_i\right)>\rho\left(X_j, Y_i\right) \text { for all } j \neq i .$$\end{document}
See Fig 1B for an example of a Y-perfect (but not X-perfect) match.

A perfect match (either X-perfect or Y-perfect) provides especially strong evidence to support the hypothesis that X and Y are dependent. With a perfect match, the original correlation ρorig is strictly greater than any of the shuffled correlations, as illustrated in Fig 1C. Thus, a perfect match guarantees pperm = 1/n!, the lowest p-value achievable with the permutation test (Fig 1D). Furthermore, letting P denote probability, we can prove that
P(X-perfect match)≤1/nn \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(X \text {-perfect match }) \leq 1 / n^n$$\end{document}
under the null hypothesis H0 wherein (1) the Yi trials are iid, (2) the Xi trials are iid, and (3) X and Y are independent (Lemma 8 in Appendix 2). In Proposition 14 (Appendix 2), we show that this upper bound of 1/nn cannot be reduced further without requiring additional assumptions.

The same bound applies to the Y-perfect match: under H0 we have P (Y −perfect match) ≤ 1/nn (Lemma 6 in Appendix 2) and thus
(1)
P(X-perfect match orY-perfect match)≤2/nn. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(X \text {-perfect match or } Y \text {-perfect match }) \leq 2 / n^n.$$\end{document}

We estimated the velocity of each fish as
v→i,t=p→i,t+1−p→i,t−12R \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \vec{v}_{i, t}=\frac{\vec{p}_{i, t+1}-\vec{p}_{i, t-1}}{2} R$$\end{document}
where p→i,t\begin{document}$\vec{p}_{i, t}$\end{document} is a 2-dimensional column vector specifying the position of the ith fish in units of body length at video frame t, v→i,t\begin{document}$\vec{v}_{i, t}$\end{document} is a 2-dimensional column vector specifying the fish velocity in units of body length per second at video frame t, and R is the frame rate (32 frames per second). The 3 trials contained similar but slightly different numbers of frames. We truncated the v→i,t\begin{document}$\vec{v}_{i, t}$\end{document} time series to the shortest among them (19308 frames ≈ 10.06 minutes) so they could be compared before downstream analyses.

A group’s average individual speed at each time was then given by
1N∑i=1N|v→i,t| \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N}\left|\vec{v}_{i, t}\right|$$\end{document}
where N is the number of fish. The mean resultant length Ct of a group at each time was given by the formula
(2)
Ct=1N|∑i=1Nv→i,t|v→i,t|| \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C_{t}=\frac{1}{N}\left|\sum_{i=1}^{N} \frac{\vec{v}_{i, t}}{\left|\vec{v}_{i, t}\right|}\right| \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Velocity vectors v→i,t\begin{document}$\vec{v}_{i, t}$\end{document} covering missing tracking data (fewer than 0.02% of such vectors in any trial) were excluded from calculations of average individual speed and mean resultant length. When calculating mean resultant length, fish with speed of exactly zero (whose direction of motion is undefined) were also excluded. Instances of zero speed were rare, occurring in at most 1 fish-frame in any trial. Average individual speed and mean resultant length were determined from the remaining fish (always at least 8 of 10), so no time point was dropped from any trial.

statement.p:
Let the statistic T be a function from Ƶ to ℝ. Given some z ∈ Ƶ, let the ordered values of {T (h1z), …, T (hmz)} be
T(1)(z)≤T(2)(z)≤⋯≤T(m)(z) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle T^{(1)}(z) \leq T^{(2)}(z) \leq \cdots \leq T^{(m)}(z)$$\end{document}
Choose some significance level α ∈ [0, 1] and let the rank k be
(S1)
k=m−⌊mα⌋ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} k=m-\lfloor m \alpha\rfloor \tag{S1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where ⌊mα⌋ is the largest integer less than or equal to mα. Then,
P(T(Z)>T(k)(Z))≤α \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P\left(T(Z)>T^{(k)}(Z)\right) \leq \alpha$$\end{document}
where P (·) denotes the probability of an event.

{h1Z,h2Z}={h1h1Z,h2h1Z}={h1h2Z,h2h2Z}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left\{h_{1} Z, h_{2} Z\right\}=\left\{h_{1} h_{1} Z, h_{2} h_{1} Z\right\}=\left\{h_{1} h_{2} Z, h_{2} h_{2} Z\right\}$$\end{document}



If Z = 0, then the above expression becomes


{1,0}={0,1}={1,0}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \{1,0\}=\{0,1\}=\{1,0\}$$\end{document}



and if Z = 1, then it is


{0,1}={1,0}={0,1}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \{0,1\}=\{1,0\}=\{0,1\}$$\end{document}



and since these are unordered sets, they are all equal, so we have verified that H satisfies requirement (2). Next, we need a function T. For example, choose T (z) = z + 10. Thus, for z = 0 we have {T (h1z), T (h2z)} = {11, 10}. The lemma then introduces a ranking notation where T (i)(Z) is the ith smallest term in the sequence. This notation is useful as we will be dealing with the set {T (h1Z), …, T (hmZ)} for potentially large values of m. Returning to our example, if z = 0, then T (1)(0) = 10 and T (2)(0) = 11. Similarly for z = 1, T (1)(1) = 10 and T (2)(1) = 11. Also, in the statement of the lemma, T (i) is defined as a function of z, not Z. This is to emphasize that the ordering depends on the particular outcome of the random variable and is not fixed. Finally, the lemma makes a claim about the probability that T (Z) exceeds T (k)(Z), which is the kth lowest statistic in the set. In the example, if α = 0.05, then k = 2 − ⌊ 0.1 ⌋ = 2. T (2)(Z) is always going to be the second smallest, i.e. the largest, element of T (h1z), T (h2z), which is 11. Of course, the probability of T (Z) being even greater than the largest number is 0, and since 0 is less than α = 0.05 we have verified the lemma in this case. As another example, suppose α = 0.5. Then k = 2 − ⌊1⌋ = 1. T (k)(Z) is then T (1)(Z), the smallest element of {T (h1z), T (h2z)}. The probability of T (Z) being greater than the smallest number is 0.5 ≤ α = 0.5, which verifies the lemma for α = 0.5.

First suppose that T (k)(Z) is not tied with any other T (i)(Z). Then Eq. S1 implies that there are ⌊ mα⌋ values of T (i)(Z) that are greater than T (k)(Z). That is,
⌊mα⌋=∑i=1mI(T(hiZ)>T(k)(Z))=∑i=1mI(T(hiZ)>T(k)(hiZ)). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned}m \alpha\rfloor & =\sum_{i=1}^{m} I\left(T\left(h_{i} Z\right)>T^{(k)}(Z)\right) \\ & =\sum_{i=1}^{m} I\left(T\left(h_{i} Z\right)>T^{(k)}\left(h_{i} Z\right)\right) .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
To arrive at the second equality, we used the fact that T (k)(Z) = T (k)(hiZ), which follows from the second requirement of the lemma (i.e. {h1Z, …, hmZ} = {h1hiZ, …, hmhiZ}).

Alternatively, if there is possibly a tie for T (k)(Z) (meaning that there may be some j ≠ k such that T (j)(Z) = T (k)(Z)), then we have a similar formula, but with an inequality instead of an equality:


⌊mα⌋≥∑i=1mI(T(hiZ)>T(k)(hiZ))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle m \alpha\rfloor \geq \sum_{i=1}^{m} I\left(T\left(h_{i} Z\right)>T^{(k)}\left(h_{i} Z\right)\right)$$\end{document}



This version with the inequality is of course general since it is true under both cases. Using this formula,


mα≥⌊mα⌋=E[⌊mα⌋]≥∑i=1mE[I(T(hiZ)>T(k)(hiZ))].
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle m \alpha \geq\lfloor m \alpha\rfloor=E[\lfloor m \alpha\rfloor] \geq \sum_{i=1}^{m} E\left[I\left(T\left(h_{i} Z\right)>T^{(k)}\left(h_{i} Z\right)\right)\right].$$\end{document}



Since Z and hiZ have the same distribution, we may remove the hi in the above formula, and we have


mα≥∑i=1mE[I(T(Z)>T(k)(Z))]=mE[I(T(Z)>T(k)(Z))].
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle m \alpha \geq \sum_{i=1}^{m} E\left[I\left(T(Z)>T^{(k)}(Z)\right)\right]=m E\left[I\left(T(Z)>T^{(k)}(Z)\right)\right].$$\end{document}



Dividing through by m gives


α≥E[I(T(Z)>T(k)(Z))]=P(T(Z)>T(k)(Z))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \alpha \geq E\left[I\left(T(Z)>T^{(k)}(Z)\right)\right]=P\left(T(Z)>T^{(k)}(Z)\right)$$\end{document}



as required.

Let X = (X1, …, Xn) and Y = (Y1, …, Yn) be sequences of random variables. Let G = {g1, …, gn!} be the complete set of functions that permute a sequence of length n. For example, if Y is (2, 7, 4), then giY might be (2, 4, 7). Define the statistic T (X, Y) to be a function that returns a real number, typically interpreted as the overall correlation strength. Repeatedly compute the statistic after permuting the elements of Y. That is, compute


{T(X,g1Y),T(X,g2Y),…,T(X,gn!Y)}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left\{T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{1} \boldsymbol{Y}\right), T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{2} \boldsymbol{Y}\right), \ldots, T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{n!} \boldsymbol{Y}\right)\right\}$$\end{document}



Let N≥ be the number of permuted statistic values that are at least as large as the original statistic value. That is,


N≥=∑i=1n!I(T(X,giY)≥T(X,Y))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle N_{\geq}=\sum_{i=1}^{n!} I\left(T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{i} \boldsymbol{Y}\right) \geq T(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})\right)$$\end{document}



where I(A) is the indicator function for the event A (meaning that I(A) = 1 if A occurs and I(A) = 0 otherwise). Then the p-value of the test is given by


pperm=N≥/n!
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle p_{\text {perm }}=N_{\geq} / n!$$\end{document}

We now prove the result directly. Below, A ↔ B means “A if and only if B”.
pperm≤α↔N≥≤n!α↔∑i=1n!I(T(X,giY)≥T(X,Y))≤n!α↔∑i=1n!(1−I(T(X,giY)≥T(X,Y)))≥n!−n!α↔∑i=1n!(I(T(X,giY)<T(X,Y)))≥n!−n!α↔∑i=1n!(I(T(X,giY)<T(X,Y)))≥n!−⌊n!α⌋. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} p_{\text {perm }} \leq \alpha & \leftrightarrow N_{\geq} \leq n!\alpha \leftrightarrow \sum_{i=1}^{n!} I\left(T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{i} \boldsymbol{Y}\right) \geq T(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})\right) \leq n!\alpha \\ & \leftrightarrow \sum_{i=1}^{n!}\left(1-I\left(T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{i} \boldsymbol{Y}\right) \geq T(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})\right)\right) \geq n!-n!\alpha \\ & \leftrightarrow \sum_{i=1}^{n!}\left(I\left(T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{i} \boldsymbol{Y}\right)<T(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})\right)\right) \geq n!-n!\alpha \\ & \leftrightarrow \sum_{i=1}^{n!}\left(I\left(T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{i} \boldsymbol{Y}\right)<T(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})\right)\right) \geq n!-\lfloor n!\alpha\rfloor .\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
As in Eq. S1, let us define k = m − ⌊ mα ⌋, where n! = m. Then, the final line above says “the number of permuted Y s that produce a smaller T than the original Y is greater than or equal to k.” Said another way, “when we rank the T s from least to greatest, the T from the original Y will have a higher rank than k”. This in turn is equivalent to T (X, Y) >T (k)(X, Y). Thus, we may directly apply Lemma 1, thereby obtaining
P(pperm≤α)=P(T(X,Y)>T(k)(X,Y))≤α \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P\left(p_{\text {perm }} \leq \alpha\right)=P\left(T(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})>T^{(k)}(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})\right) \leq \alpha$$\end{document}
as required.

We proceed by showing that the permutation test where Y is permuted is equivalent to the permutation test where X is permuted. Notice that to show this equivalence it is sufficient to establish that


{T(X,g1Y),…,T(X,gn!Y)}={T(g1X,Y),…,T(gn!X,Y)}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left\{T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{1} \boldsymbol{Y}\right), \ldots, T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{n!}^{\boldsymbol{Y}}\right)\right\}=\left\{T\left(g_{1} \boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}\right), \ldots, T\left(g_{n!} \boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}\right)\right\}$$\end{document}



where the left and right sides both denote unordered sets. It will be useful to refer to the inverse permutation gi−1\begin{document}$g_{i}^{-1}$\end{document}, which is the permutation that “undoes” gi. More precisely, gi−1\begin{document}$g_{i}^{-1}$\end{document} is defined by the relation gi−1giZ=Z\begin{document}$g_{i}^{-1} g_{i} Z=Z$\end{document}. Using Eq. S2, we have:

(S3)

T(X,giY)=T(gi−1X,gi−1giY)=T(gi−1X,Y).
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{i} \boldsymbol{Y}\right) & =T\left(g_{i}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}, g_{i}^{-1} g_{i} \boldsymbol{Y}\right)  \tag{S3}\\ & =T\left(g_{i}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}\right) . \end{align*}$$\end{document}



Note that the inverse permutation gi−1\begin{document}$g_{i}^{-1}$\end{document} is guaranteed to exist because any permutation gi can be “undone” by some other permutation (gi−1)\begin{document}$\left(g_{i}^{-1}\right)$\end{document}. In the edge case where gi is the trivial identity permutation, gi−1\begin{document}$g_{i}^{-1}$\end{document} is also the identity permutation. Additionally, the set of permutation functions is the same as the set of inverse permutation functions, meaning that

(S4)

{g1,…,gn!}={g1−1,…,gn!−1}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \left\{g_{1}, \ldots, g_{n!}\right\}=\left\{g_{1}^{-1}, \ldots, g_{n!}^{-1}\right\} \tag{S4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



because each permutation is an inverse permutation (because gi inverts gi−1\begin{document}$g_{i}^{-1}$\end{document}), and each inverse permutation is a permutation. It follows that


{T(X,g1Y),…,T(X,gn!Y)}={T(g1−1X,Y),…,T(gn!−1X,Y)}={T(g1X,Y),…,T(gn!X,Y)}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned}\left\{T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{1} \boldsymbol{Y}\right), \ldots, T\left(\boldsymbol{X}, g_{n!} \boldsymbol{Y}\right)\right\} & =\left\{T\left(g_{1}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}\right), \ldots, T\left(g_{n!}^{-1} \boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}\right)\right\} \\ & =\left\{T\left(g_{1} \boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}\right), \ldots, T\left(g_{n!} \boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}\right)\right\}\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



where the first equality follows from Eq. S3 and the second equality follows from Eq. S4. This shows that the permutation test where Y is permuted is equivalent to the permutation test where X is permuted.

Let X = (X1, …, Xn) and Y = (Y1, …, Yn) be sequences of random variables. Let ρ (the “correlation function”) be a function that maps a pair (Xi, Yj) to a real number. We say that the event MY (also called a “Y-perfect match”) occurs if and only if:
ρ(Xi,Yi)>ρ(Xj,Yi) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \rho\left(X_{i}, Y_{i}\right)>\rho\left(X_{j}, Y_{i}\right)$$\end{document}
for all pairs (i, j) such that i≠ j.

Similarly, the event MX (also called an “X-perfect match”) occurs if and only if
ρ(Xi,Yi)>ρ(Xi,Yj) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \rho\left(X_{i}, Y_{i}\right)>\rho\left(X_{i}, Y_{j}\right)$$\end{document}
for all pairs (i, j) such that i≠ j.

Proof: Let X and Y be the sample spaces of X and Y (i.e. X ∈ X and Y ∈ Y). Let c be a function that maps from X × Y to ℕn. Specifically, c(x, y) = (c1(x, y), …, cn(x, y)), where each ci is given as follows:
ci(x,y)=argmaxj(ρ(xj,yi)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle c_{i}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{y})=\underset{j}{\operatorname{argmax}}\left(\rho\left(x_{j}, y_{i}\right)\right)$$\end{document}
That is, ci(x, y) ∈ {1, …, n} is defined to be the index j that maximizes ρ(xj, yi). In the case of a multi-way tie, ci is the lowest from among the maximizing index values. (For example, if j = 3 and j = 7 both produced the greatest value of ρ(xj, yi), then ci would be 3 since 3 < 7.) Note that if a Y-perfect match occurs, then
c1(X,Y)=1,…,cn(X,Y)=n \begin{document}$$\displaystyle c_{1}(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})=1, \ldots, c_{n}(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})=n$$\end{document}
Consider c(x, Y) for a fixed x ∈ X. Notice that
c1(x,Y),…,cn(x,Y) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle c_{1}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y}), \ldots, c_{n}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})$$\end{document}
are iid because each ci(x, Y) depends only on Yi. Since the ci(x, Y) terms are identically distributed across i, we have:
P(ci(x,Y)=i)=P(c1(x,Y)=i) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P\left(c_{i}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=i\right)=P\left(c_{1}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=i\right)$$\end{document}
for all i. Therefore we have
∑i=1nP(ci(x,Y)=i)=∑i=1nP(c1(x,Y)=i)=1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n} P\left(c_{i}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=i\right)=\sum_{i=1}^{n} P\left(c_{1}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=i\right)=1$$\end{document}
By dividing through by n, we may write
(S5)
1n=1n∑i=1nP(ci(x,Y)=i)≥(∏i=1nP(ci(x,Y)=i))1/n \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{1}{n}=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} P\left(c_{i}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=i\right) \geq\left(\prod_{i=1}^{n} P\left(c_{i}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=i\right)\right)^{1 / n} \tag{S5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where the inequality comes from the AM-GM inequality (e.g., [43] pg. 20). Since the ci(x, Y) terms are independent,
∏i=1nP(ci(x,Y)=i)=P(c1(x,Y)=1,…,cn(x,Y)=n) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{n} P\left(c_{i}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=i\right)=P\left(c_{1}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=1, \ldots, c_{n}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=n\right)$$\end{document}
Substituting this fact into inequality S5 and raising both sides to the nth power gives us
(S6)
1nn≥P(c1(x,Y)=1,…,cn(x,Y)=n)=P(c(x,Y)=[1,…,n]) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{1}{n^{n}} \geq P\left(c_{1}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=1, \ldots, c_{n}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=n\right)=P(\boldsymbol{c}(\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y})=[1, \ldots, n]) \tag{S6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Since the above inequality holds for all x ∈ X, and since X and Y are independent, Lemma 7 (given immediately following this proof) implies
(S7)
P(c(X,Y)=[1,…,n])≤1nn \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} P(\boldsymbol{c}(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})=[1, \ldots, n]) \leq \frac{1}{n^{n}} \tag{S7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Since a Y-perfect match implies c(X, Y) = [1, …, n], the left side of this inequality has a lower bound of P (MY), giving us
P(MY)≤P(c(X,Y)=[1,…,n])≤1nn \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P\left(M_{Y}\right) \leq P(\boldsymbol{c}(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})=[1, \ldots, n]) \leq \frac{1}{n^{n}}$$\end{document}
as required.

P(f(x,Y)=v)≤pfor allx∈X
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(f(x, Y)=v) \leq p \text { for all } x \in \mathcal{X}$$\end{document}



Then,


P(f(X,Y)=v)≤p
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(f(X, Y)=v) \leq p$$\end{document}



Proof: Let Iv(x, y) denote the indicator function for the event where f (x, y) = v. That is, Iv(x, y) = 1 if f (x, y) = v and otherwise Iv(x, y) = 0.

From the law of total expectation we have
(S8)
P(f(X,Y)=v)=E[Iv(X,Y)]=E[E(Iv(X,Y)∣X)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} P(f(X, Y)=v) & =E\left[I_{v}(X, Y)\right] \\ & =E\left[E\left(I_{v}(X, Y) \mid X\right)\right] \tag{S8} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Let g(x) be a function given by
g(x)=E[Iv(x,Y)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle g(x)=E\left[I_{v}(x, Y)\right]$$\end{document}
Since X and Y are independent, it follows (e.g., see Example 4.1.7 in [44]) that g(X) = E(Iv(X, Y) X). Substituting this fact into equation S8 gives
P(f(X,Y)=v)=E[g(X)]. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(f(X, Y)=v)=E[g(X)].$$\end{document}
Note that it follows from the conditions of this lemma that g(x) ≤ p for all x ∈ X. Thus, monotonicity of the expected value implies E[g(X)] ≤ E[p] = p. Combining this fact with the above equation, we obtain
P(f(X,Y)=v)≤p \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(f(X, Y)=v) \leq p$$\end{document}
as required.

Suppose a Y-perfect match occurs, meaning that
ρ(Xi,Yi)>ρ(Xj,Yi)∀(i≠j) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \rho\left(X_{i}, Y_{i}\right)>\rho\left(X_{j}, Y_{i}\right) \forall(i \neq j)$$\end{document}
In other words, for each Yi, its correlation term ρ(, Yi) is strictly maximized when Yi is paired with Xi. Applying a permutation to Y (other than the trivial identity permutation) means that for at least one choice of i, the ρ(Xi, Yi) term in Eq. S9 becomes replaced with ρ(Xj, Yi) for some j ≠ i. But ρ(Xi, Yi) >ρ(Xj, Yi). Therefore, T (X, Y) must be strictly greater than all permuted versions T (X, giY) (other than when gi is the identity permutation). It follows that pperm = 1/n!.

For the inductive step, suppose the target inequality 1/n! > 2/nn holds. Multiplying the target inequality on both sides by 1/(n + 1), we obtain
1(n+1)!>2nn(1n+1)>2(n+1)n(1n+1)=2(n+1)n+1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{1}{(n+1)!}>\frac{2}{n^{n}}\left(\frac{1}{n+1}\right)>\frac{2}{(n+1)^{n}}\left(\frac{1}{n+1}\right)=\frac{2}{(n+1)^{n+1}}$$\end{document}
This shows that 1/n! > 2/nn implies 1/(n + 1)! > 2/(n + 1)n+1. We conclude by the inductive principle that the target inequality is true for n > 2.

Let X = (X1, …, Xn) and Y = (Y1, …, Yn) be sequences of iid random variables. Let X and Y be independent. Let the correlation function ρ be a function that maps a pair (Xi, Yj) to a real number. Define the average correlation
T(X,Y)=1n∑i=1nρ(Xi,Yi) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle T(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y})=\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n} \rho\left(X_{i}, Y_{i}\right)$$\end{document}
Perform the permutation test of independence (Def. 2) using T as the statistic, and obtain the p-value pperm. Also check for an X-perfect match test and a Y-perfect match test (Def. 5) using ρ as the correlation function. Let
p={1/nnif anX-perfect match occurs2/nnif aY-perfect match occurs, but not anX-perfect matchppermif neither occurs \begin{document}$$\displaystyle p= \begin{cases}1 / n^{n} & \text { if an } X \text {-perfect match occurs } \\ 2 / n^{n} & \text { if a } Y \text {-perfect match occurs, but not an } X \text {-perfect match } \\ p_{\text {perm }} & \text { if neither occurs }\end{cases}$$\end{document}
Then P (p ≤ α) ≤ α for α ∈ [0, 1] and n > 1.

Case i: Suppose α ≥ 1/n!. In this case, p ≤ α if MX occurs or MY occurs or pperm ≤ α.


P(p≤α)=P(pperm≤αorMXorMY)=P(pperm≤αor(MXorMY))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P(p \leq \alpha) & =P\left(p_{\text {perm }} \leq \alpha \text { or } M_{X} \text { or } M_{Y}\right) \\ & =P\left(p_{\text {perm }} \leq \alpha \text { or }\left(M_{X} \text { or } M_{Y}\right)\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



This may be split into 3 terms via the identity P (A or B) = P (A and B)+P (A and not B)+P ((not A) and B), as follows:


P(p≤α)=P(pperm≤αand(MXorMY))+P(pperm≤αand not(MXorMY))+P(pperm>αand(MXorMY))
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P(p \leq \alpha) & =P\left(p_{\text {perm }} \leq \alpha \text { and }\left(M_{X} \text { or } M_{Y}\right)\right) \\ & +P\left(p_{\text {perm }} \leq \alpha \text { and not }\left(M_{X} \text { or } M_{Y}\right)\right) \\ & +P\left(p_{\text {perm }}>\alpha \text { and }\left(M_{X} \text { or } M_{Y}\right)\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



However, by Lemma 9, if either MX or MY occurs, then pperm = 1/n!, which would in turn imply pperm ≤ α under case i. That is, the term P (pperm >α and (MX or MY)) in the above summation is zero. Removing this term, we have


P(p≤α)=P(pperm≤αand(MXorMY))+P(pperm≤αand not(MXorMY))=P(pperm≤α)≤α
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P(p \leq \alpha) & =P\left(p_{\text {perm }} \leq \alpha \text { and }\left(M_{X} \text { or } M_{Y}\right)\right)+P\left(p_{\text {perm }} \leq \alpha \text { and not }\left(M_{X} \text { or } M_{Y}\right)\right) \\ & =P\left(p_{\text {perm }} \leq \alpha\right) \leq \alpha\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



where the final inequality is due to Lemma 3. This establishes the claim for case i.

Case ii: Suppose 1/n! >α ≥ 2/nn. In this case, pperm >α since pperm cannot be less than 1/n!, so we have
P(p≤α)=P(MXorMY)≤P(MX)+P(MY) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P(p \leq \alpha) & =P\left(M_{X} \text { or } M_{Y}\right) \\ & \leq P\left(M_{X}\right)+P\left(M_{Y}\right)\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
From Lemmas 6-8 we know P (MX) ≤ 1/nn and P (MY) ≤ 1/nn. Thus the above inequality becomes
P(p≤α)≤P(MX)+P(MY)≤1/nn+1/nn≤α \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(p \leq \alpha) \leq P\left(M_{X}\right)+P\left(M_{Y}\right) \leq 1 / n^{n}+1 / n^{n} \leq \alpha$$\end{document}
which establishes the claim in case ii.

Case iii: Suppose 2/nn >α ≥ 1/nn. In this case, only MX will give us p ≤ α. So we have
P(p≤α)=P(MX)≤1/nn≤α \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(p \leq \alpha)=P\left(M_{X}\right) \leq 1 / n^{n} \leq \alpha$$\end{document}
which verifies the claim in case iii.

Consider the same setting as Theorem 11, except that now
p={1/nnif aY-perfect match occurs2/nnif anX-perfect match occurs, but not aY-perfect matchppermif neither occurs \begin{document}$$\displaystyle p= \begin{cases}1 / n^{n} & \text { if a } Y \text {-perfect match occurs } \\ 2 / n^{n} & \text { if an } X \text {-perfect match occurs, but not a } Y \text {-perfect match } \\ p_{\text {perm }} & \text { if neither occurs }\end{cases}$$\end{document}
Then, P (p ≤ α) ≤ α for α ∈ [0, 1] and n > 1.

Consider the same setting as Theorem 11, except that now
q={2/nnif anX-perfect match orY-perfect match occursppermif neither occurs \begin{document}$$\displaystyle q= \begin{cases}2 / n^{n} & \text { if an } X \text {-perfect match or } Y \text {-perfect match occurs } \\ p_{\text {perm }} & \text { if neither occurs }\end{cases}$$\end{document}
Then, P (q ≤ α) ≤ α for α ∈ [0, 1] and n > 1.

Then it follows from Theorem 11 that
P(q≤α)≤P(p≤α)≤α \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(q \leq \alpha) \leq P(p \leq \alpha) \leq \alpha$$\end{document}
so P (q ≤ α) ≤ α as required.

For each j ∈ {1, …, n} and each l ∈ {1, …, k}, let
Yj,l∼Uniform(0,1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle Y_{j, l} \sim \operatorname{Uniform}(0,1)$$\end{document}
where all Yj,l terms are independent of each other and of the Xi terms. Define Yj = [Yj,1, …, Yj,k]. In the story above, Xi is the dimension that student i cares about, and Yj,l is the quality of advisor j along dimension l.

Let the correlation function be


ρ(Xi,Yj)=Yj,Xi
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \rho\left(X_{i}, Y_{j}\right)=Y_{j, X_{i}}$$\end{document}



In this case, we say that an X-perfect match occurs if and only if


Yi,Xi>Yj,Xifor all pairs(i,j)such thatj≠i
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle Y_{i, X_i}>Y_{j, X_i} \text { for all pairs }(i, j) \text { such that } j \neq i$$\end{document}

Proof: Fix x ∈ {1, …, k} and let Aj,x be the event that Yj,x is the strictly largest element from the sequence Y[:,x] = [Y1,x, …, Yn,x]. Since the elements of Y[:,x] are iid continuous random variables, the probability of a “tie” for largest is zero, and so it follows from symmetry that P (Aj,x) = 1/n for all j. Now, fix {x1, …, xn} where each xi is unique. Then, Ai,xi\begin{document}$A_{i, x_{i}}$\end{document} are all independent of one another because each Ai,xi\begin{document}$A_{i, x_{i}}$\end{document} depends only on Y[:,xi]\begin{document}$Y_{\left[:, x_{i}\right]}$\end{document}, and the Y[:,xi]\begin{document}$Y_{\left[:, x_{i}\right]}$\end{document} are independent. Thus


P(⋂i=1nAi,xi)=∏i=1nP(Ai,xi)=1/nn
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P\left(\bigcap_{i=1}^{n} A_{i, x_{i}}\right)=\prod_{i=1}^{n} P\left(A_{i, x_{i}}\right)=1 / n^{n}$$\end{document}



Notice that for X = x, an X-perfect match occurs if and only if all the Ai,xi\begin{document}$A_{i, x_{i}}$\end{document} events occur. Let us formalize this further. Let MX be a set in the product sample space of X and Y such that (x, y) ∈ MX if and only if yi,xi>yj,xi\begin{document}$y_{i, x_{i}}>y_{j, x_{i}}$\end{document} for all pairs i≠ j. Moreover, let us denote an X-perfect match by the symbol MX. Notice that MX occurs if and only if (X, Y) ∈ MX. Now, we may rewrite the above equation as


P((x,Y)∈MX)=1/nn
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P\left((\boldsymbol{x}, \boldsymbol{Y}) \in \mathcal{M}_{X}\right)=1 / n^{n}$$\end{document}



for all x whose entries are unique. Applying Lemma 15 (given just after the present proof) to the above result, we see that


P((X,Y)∈MX∣X1≠⋯≠Xn)=1/nn
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P\left((\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}) \in \mathcal{M}_{X} \mid X_{1} \neq \cdots \neq X_{n}\right)=1 / n^{n}$$\end{document}



We are now ready to complete the result. We start with


P((X,Y)∈MX)≥P((X1≠⋯≠Xn)⋂(X,Y)∈MX)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P\left((\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}) \in \mathcal{M}_{X}\right) \geq P\left(\left(X_{1} \neq \cdots \neq X_{n}\right) \bigcap(\boldsymbol{X}, \boldsymbol{Y}) \in \mathcal{M}_{X}\right)$$\end{document}



Substituting MX ⇐⇒ (X, Y) ∈ MX, we now obtain:

(S10)

P(MX)≥P((X1≠⋯≠Xn)⋂MX)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} P\left(M_{X}\right) \geq P\left(\left(X_{1} \neq \cdots \neq X_{n}\right) \bigcap M_{X}\right) \tag{S10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(S11)

P(MX)≥P(X1≠⋯≠Xn)P(MX∣X1≠⋯≠Xn)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} P\left(M_{X}\right) \geq P\left(X_{1} \neq \cdots \neq X_{n}\right) P\left(M_{X} \mid X_{1} \neq \cdots \neq X_{n}\right) \tag{S11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}




(S12)

P(MX)≥P(X1≠⋯≠Xn)/nn
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} P\left(M_{X}\right) \geq P\left(X_{1} \neq \cdots \neq X_{n}\right) / n^{n} \tag{S12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Since X1, …, Xn are uniformly distributed, we have

(S13)

P(X1≠⋯≠Xn)=∏i=0n−1k−ik>(1−n−1k)n
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} P\left(X_{1} \neq \cdots \neq X_{n}\right)=\prod_{i=0}^{n-1} \frac{k-i}{k}>\left(1-\frac{n-1}{k}\right)^{n} \tag{S13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



We have not yet specified k; we do so now. Choose k to be any integer with:


k≥n−11−q1/n
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle k \geq \frac{n-1}{1-q^{1 / n}}$$\end{document}



Then, combining this choice of k with inequality S13, we have P (X1 ≠ · · · ≠ Xn) > q substitute into inequality S12 to obtain P (MX) >q/nn, as required.

P((x,Y)∈B)=pfor allx∈A
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P((x, Y) \in B)=p \text { for all } x \in A$$\end{document}



Then,


P((X,Y)∈B∣X∈A)=p
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P((X, Y) \in B \mid X \in A)=p$$\end{document}



Proof: Let IB(x, y) denote the indicator function for the event where (x, y) ∈ B. That is, IB(x, y) = 1 if (x, y) ∈ B and otherwise IB(x, y) = 0. Let g(x) be a function given by


g(x)=E[IB(x,Y)]=P((x,Y)∈B)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle g(x)=E\left[I_{B}(x, Y)\right]=P((x, Y) \in B)$$\end{document}



Note that it follows from the conditions of this lemma that g(x) = p for all x ∈ A. Consider the joint probability


P((X,Y)∈B,X∈A)=E[IB(X,Y)IA(X)]
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P((X, Y) \in B, X \in A)=E\left[I_{B}(X, Y) I_{A}(X)\right]$$\end{document}



where IA(x) is the indicator function for x ∈ A. From the law of total expectation, the above is equal to


=E[E(IB(X,Y)IA(X)∣X)]
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle =E\left[E\left(I_{B}(X, Y) I_{A}(X) \mid X\right)\right]$$\end{document}



since IA(X) is a function of X only, we may pull it out of the inner conditional expectation (“taking out what is known”; see for instance Theorem 9.3.2 in [45]):

(S14)

=E[IA(X)E(IB(X,Y)∣X)]
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} =E\left[I_{A}(X) E\left(I_{B}(X, Y) \mid X\right)\right] \tag{S14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}



Since X and Y are independent, it follows from Example 4.1.7 in [44] that g(X) = E(IB(X, Y) | X).

Substituting this result into Eq. S14 gives


=E[IA(X)g(X)]
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle =E\left[I_{A}(X) g(X)\right]$$\end{document}



Notice that IA(x)g(x) = IA(x)p because both evaluate to p for all x ∈ A, and evaluate to 0 for all x /∈ A. Thus the above becomes


=pE[IA(X)]
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle =p E\left[I_{A}(X)\right]$$\end{document}



To summarize this chain of equalities, we now have


P((X,Y)∈B,X∈A)=pE[IA(X)]=pP(X∈A)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle P((X, Y) \in B, X \in A)=p E\left[I_{A}(X)\right]=p P(X \in A)$$\end{document}



Finally, by substituting this result into the definition of conditional probability, we have


P((X,Y)∈B∣X∈A)=P((X,Y)∈B,X∈A)P(X∈A)=pP(X∈A)P(X∈A)=p
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P((X, Y) \in B \mid X \in A) & =\frac{P((X, Y) \in B, X \in A)}{P(X \in A)} \\ & =\frac{p P(X \in A)}{P(X \in A)}=p\end{aligned}$$\end{document}



as required.

sec.p:
Each joint probability is of course bounded between 0 and 1. Thus, moving clockwise from the upper left of the 2 × 2 inner table we have the following constraints on P′:
0≤α/2+P′≤10≤1−α−P′≤10≤P′≤10≤α/2−P′≤1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} & 0 \leq \alpha / 2+P^{\prime} \leq 1 \\ & 0 \leq 1-\alpha-P^{\prime} \leq 1 \\ & 0 \leq P^{\prime} \leq 1 \\ & 0 \leq \alpha / 2-P^{\prime} \leq 1\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
Among these constraints, since α < 1/2, the tightest bounds on P′ are 0 ≤ P′ ≤ α/2. Notice that the false positive rate of the overall procedure is given by:
(S16)
P(p≤α)=1−P(pX>αand2pY>α)=α+P′ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} P(p \leq \alpha) & =1-P\left(p_{X}>\alpha \text { and } 2 p_{Y}>\alpha\right) \\ & =\alpha+P^{\prime} \tag{S16} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
Fig S1 plots Eq S16 within the allowed bounds of P′, noting the extreme cases of P′ = 0 and P′ = α/2, as well as the case of independence in which P′ = P (2pY ≤ α) × (pX >α). Other than the extreme case of P′ = 0, in which a significant Y test result deterministically ensures that the X test is also significant, the false positive rate of the overall procedure, P (p ≤ α), will be greater than α. Thus the procedure is not valid.

statement.p:
(S17)
p={1/nnifMXoccurs2/nnifMYoccurs, but notMX1neither occurs \begin{document}$$\displaystyle p=\left\{\begin{array}{cc} 1 / n^{n} & \text { if } M_{X} \text { occurs } \tag{S17}\\ 2 / n^{n} & \text { if } M_{Y} \text { occurs, but not } M_{X} \\ 1 & \text { neither occurs } \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
Then, P (p ≤ α) ≤ α for 0 ≤ α ≤ 1 and for all integers n ≥ 2.

Second is the case 1 >α ≥ 2/nn. In this case, either MX or MY is sufficient to achieve p ≤ α. So we have:
P(p≤α)=P(MXorMY)≤P(MX)+P(MY)≤2/nn≤α \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P(p \leq \alpha) & =P\left(M_{X} \text { or } M_{Y}\right) \\ & \leq P\left(M_{X}\right)+P\left(M_{Y}\right) \leq 2 / n^{n} \leq \alpha\end{aligned}$$\end{document}
as required.

Third, consider the case 2/nn >α ≥ 1/nn. In this case, only MX will give us p ≤ α. So we have
P(p≤α)=P(MX)≤1/nn≤α \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P(p \leq \alpha)=P\left(M_{X}\right) \leq 1 / n^{n} \leq \alpha$$\end{document}
as required.
